# IR Remote

ESP32-based IR blaster. The ESP32 exposes a WiFi HTTP server and a USB serial
interface; Python scripts on the host record and replay signals.

## Hardware

- ESP32
- IR LED on GPIO 2 (send)
- IR receiver on GPIO 4 (receive)

## Firmware

Flash with PlatformIO:

```bash
cd platformio_project
pio run --target upload
```

Once connected the ESP32 is reachable at `http://ir-remote.local` (mDNS) or
its IP address. It also responds on the USB serial port at 115200 baud.

Opening that address in a browser shows a control page with buttons for the
saved signals. The page is `remote.html` in the repo root; it is baked into
the firmware at build time, so re-run the upload after editing it.

WiFi credentials are stored in NVS flash and persist across reboots. The
compiled-in defaults are used on first boot; after that use `wifi_setup.py`
or the `/wifi` HTTP endpoint to update them without reflashing.

## Transport

All Python scripts accept `--serial` or `--port` to switch from WiFi to USB:

```bash
python3 record.py                        # WiFi (default)
python3 record.py --serial               # USB serial, auto-detect port
python3 record.py --port /dev/cu.usbserial1410  # USB serial, explicit port
```

## Signal database

Signals are stored in `ir_signals.json` — one named entry per button.
Decoded signals and raw timing fallbacks coexist in the same file:

```json
{
  "tv_power":   { "protocol": "NEC",     "address": "0x4",  "command": "0x8",  "repeats": 0 },
  "hifi_power": { "protocol": "SAMSUNG", "address": "0x7",  "command": "0x40", "repeats": 1 },
  "odd_button": { "raw": [9000, 4500, 560, 1690, 560, 560], "repeats": 0 }
}
```

Supported decoded protocols: NEC, SAMSUNG, SONY, RC5, RC6, LG, PANASONIC.
Buttons that use an unknown protocol are stored as raw microsecond timings.

## Scripts

### wifi_setup.py — update WiFi credentials

Updates the WiFi SSID and password stored in NVS flash without reflashing the
firmware. The ESP32 reconnects immediately; the new credentials persist across
reboots.

```bash
python3 wifi_setup.py                        # auto-detect port, prompt for credentials
python3 wifi_setup.py MyNetwork              # prompt for password only
python3 wifi_setup.py MyNetwork MyPassword   # pass everything inline
python3 wifi_setup.py --port /dev/cu.usbserial1410 MyNetwork MyPassword
```

The password is hidden when prompted interactively. Credentials can also be
updated over WiFi:

```bash
curl -X POST "http://ir-remote.local/wifi?ssid=MyNetwork&pass=MyPassword"
```

### record.py — capture signals

```bash
python3 record.py
```

Point the remote at the ESP32 and press each button when prompted. Enter `list`
to see recorded signals, `quit` to exit. If the protocol is unrecognised the
script automatically falls back to raw capture.

```bash
python3 record.py --raw   # always capture raw timings, skip protocol decode
```

### play.py — replay signals

```bash
python3 play.py list                             # list all recorded signals
python3 play.py send tv_power                    # send a single signal
python3 play.py sequence tv_power tv_hdmi1 tv_vol_up tv_vol_up
python3 play.py sequence tv_power tv_hdmi1 --delay 1.5  # custom gap (seconds)
```

### ir_control.py — low-level scripting

```bash
python3 ir_control.py ping                              # check connectivity
python3 ir_control.py send NEC 0x4 0x2 --repeats 1     # send raw signal
python3 ir_control.py listen                            # print received signals
```

### md.py — Minidisc player control

Interactive menu for controlling a MDS-S40 Minidisc player over IR. Handles T9-style
text entry for track naming, sending entire keystroke sequences to the ESP32
in one request so the device executes them locally.
This is specifically designed for the MDS-S40 Minidisc player (it's what I got) but
can easily be adapted for other players that use an IR remote.

```bash
python3 md.py
python3 md.py --serial      # use USB serial transport
```

**Menu keys**

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| `n` | Enter naming mode (`md-name` signal) |
| `t` | Type text (T9 encoding, sent as one sequence) |
| `b` | Batch name tracks from a file |
| `o` | Power on/off |
| `]` | Next track |
| `[` | Previous track |
| `f` | FF (advance cursor / next character) |
| `r` | RW (back cursor / previous character) |
| `c` | Cycle character mode (number → upper → lower) |
| `s` | Send any signal by name |
| `+` | Increase inter-signal delay |
| `-` | Decrease inter-signal delay |
| `q` | Quit |

**Batch track naming**

Create a plain text file with one track name per line:

```
Side A
My Favourite Song
Track 3
```

Pass it on the command line to name all tracks non-interactively:

```bash
python3 md.py --names-file tracks.txt
```

Or press `b` inside the menu and enter the file path when prompted. The script
sends the entire sequence for all tracks as a single request to the ESP32,
which executes them in order with the correct timing.

**Required signals in ir_signals.json**

Record these with `record.py` before using `md.py`:

| Name | Button |
|------|--------|
| `md-name` | Name/title edit |
| `md-char` | Cycle character mode |
| `md-ff` | FF / advance cursor |
| `md-rw` | RW / back cursor |
| `md-on` | Power |
| `md-next` | Next track |
| `md-prev` | Previous track |
| `md-2` … `md-9`, `md-0` | Number / character keys |

**T9 character map**

| Key | Lower | Upper | Number |
|-----|-------|-------|--------|
| `2` | abc   | ABC   | 2 |
| `3` | def   | DEF   | 3 |
| `4` | ghi   | GHI   | 4 |
| `5` | jkl   | JKL   | 5 |
| `6` | mno   | MNO   | 6 |
| `7` | prs   | PRS   | 7 |
| `8` | tuv   | TUV   | 8 |
| `9` | wxy   | WXY   | 9 |
| `0` | qz    | QZ    | 0 |

Spaces are entered with FF. Numbers advance the cursor automatically (no FF
needed). The mode resets to lowercase at the end of each typed string.

## ESP32 HTTP API

| Method | Path               | Description |
|--------|--------------------|-------------|
| GET    | `/`                | Control page (`remote.html`) |
| GET    | `/ping`            | Health check — returns `PONG` |
| POST   | `/send`            | Send a decoded signal |
| POST   | `/send/raw`        | Send raw microsecond timings |
| GET    | `/recv`            | Poll for a decoded received signal |
| GET    | `/recv/raw`        | Poll for raw received timings |
| POST   | `/sequence`        | Queue a sequence of signals for local execution |
| GET    | `/sequence/status` | Returns `BUSY n/total` or `IDLE` |
| POST   | `/wifi`            | Update WiFi credentials in NVS and reconnect |
| POST   | `/wol`             | Broadcast a Wake on LAN magic packet — `mac=D8:43:AE:C9:AC:BA` |

### /send params

| Param | Example | Notes |
|-------|---------|-------|
| `protocol` | `NEC` | Required |
| `address` | `0x4` | Required, hex |
| `command` | `0x8` | Required, hex |
| `repeats` | `1` | Optional, default 0 |
| `value` | `0x...` | Optional, used by SONY |

### /sequence body fields

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `seq` | Pipe-delimited entries: `proto,addr,cmd,repeats[,value]`. Use `DELAY,<ms_hex>,0,0` to insert a pause. |
| `delay` | Milliseconds between IR signals (default 300) |

Returns `OK N` where N is the number of entries queued.

### curl examples

```bash
curl http://ir-remote.local/ping

curl -X POST "http://ir-remote.local/send?protocol=NEC&address=0x4&command=0x2"

curl http://ir-remote.local/recv
# → protocol=NEC&address=0x4&command=0x2&value=0x...

curl -X POST http://ir-remote.local/send/raw \
     --data "raw=9000,4500,560,1690,560,560&repeats=0"

curl -X POST http://ir-remote.local/sequence \
     --data "delay=400&seq=NEC,0x4,0x2,0|NEC,0x4,0x8,0"
```

## Dependencies

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Or with a virtual environment:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate   # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

- `requests` — WiFi HTTP transport
- `pyserial` — USB serial transport (`--serial` / `--port`)
